 # ---------- Standard English conventions: boundaries ----------
 ("Boundaries", "The library's new hours are designed for working ______ it now stays open until 10 p.m. on weekdays.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["families:", "families,", "families", "families, and,"],
  "A colon can join two complete sentences when the second explains the first. A comma alone is a comma splice."),
 ("Boundaries", "Rosalind Franklin's X-ray images of DNA ______ especially the one known as Photo 51, helped other scientists determine the molecule's double-helix shape.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["molecules,", "molecules", "molecules;", "molecules:"],
  "\"Especially the one known as Photo 51\" is an extra phrase, so it needs commas on both sides."),
 ("Boundaries", "The team tested three materials for the ______ cotton, wool and a recycled plastic fabric.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["jacket lining:", "jacket lining;", "jacket lining,", "jacket lining. These were"],
  "A colon introduces a list after a complete sentence. A semicolon can't introduce a list."),
 ("Boundaries", "Many people think the tomato is a ______ botanists classify it as a fruit because it develops from a flower and contains seeds.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["vegetable; however,", "vegetable, however,", "vegetable however", "vegetable; however"],
  "\"However\" joins two complete sentences here, so it needs a semicolon before it and a comma after it."),
 ("Boundaries", "The marathon runner, who had trained for two ______ finished the race in under three hours.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["years,", "years", "years;", "years:"],
  "\"Who had trained for two years\" is an extra clause set off by commas, so a comma must close it."),
 ("Boundaries", "When the power went out during the storm, the ______ kept the restaurant open by cooking on a gas stove and serving meals by candlelight.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["owners", "owners,", "owners;", "owners:"],
  "No punctuation belongs between a subject (\"owners\") and its verb (\"kept\")."),
 ("Boundaries", "Astronomers have discovered thousands of planets orbiting other ______ many of them are larger than Jupiter.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["stars, and", "stars,", "stars", "stars and,"],
  "Two complete sentences joined with \"and\" need a comma before \"and.\""),
 ("Boundaries", "My uncle's favorite saying is ______ \"Measure twice, cut once.\"",
  CONVENTIONS, ["simple:", "simple;", "simple,", "simple. That"],
  "A colon introduces the quotation that explains what the saying is, after a complete sentence."),
 # ---------- Standard English conventions: form, structure and sense ----------
 ("Form, structure and sense", "The results of the survey, which was sent to more than 3,000 households, ______ that most residents support the new park.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["show", "shows", "has shown", "is showing"],
  "The subject is \"results\" (plural), so the verb is \"show.\""),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "Neither the coach nor her assistants ______ expected the team to win the championship.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["had", "has", "was", "is"],
  "With \"neither... nor,\" the verb agrees with the nearer subject, \"assistants\" (plural). \"Had\" works for plural; the others are singular."),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "Each of the paintings in the gallery ______ a small card describing the artist and the year it was made.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["has", "have", "are having", "were having"],
  "\"Each\" is singular, so the verb is \"has.\""),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "When the scientists published their findings, ______ were surprised by how quickly other labs confirmed them.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["they", "it", "he", "one"],
  "The pronoun refers to \"the scientists\" (plural): \"they.\""),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "Last summer, my sister ______ a job at a veterinary clinic, where she learned to care for injured animals.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["took", "takes", "will take", "has been taking"],
  "\"Last summer\" sets the action in the past, so use the simple past: \"took.\""),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "Having studied the old maps for weeks, ______",
  CONVENTIONS, ["the researcher finally located the lost village.", "the lost village was finally located by the researcher.", "the old maps finally showed the lost village.", "finally, the lost village was found."],
  "The person who studied the maps must come right after the comma: \"the researcher.\""),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "The company's founder, along with two engineers, ______ the first prototype in a garage.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["built", "were building", "have built", "are building"],
  "\"Along with two engineers\" doesn't change the subject, \"founder.\" \"Built\" is correct for a singular past subject; the others are plural or the wrong tense."),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "The ______ decision to extend the deadline gave students an extra week to finish their projects.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["teachers'", "teacher's'", "teachers", "teacher"],
  "Several teachers made the decision, so use the plural possessive \"teachers'.\""),
 ("Form, structure and sense", "If I ______ known about the shortcut, I would have arrived on time.",
  CONVENTIONS, ["had", "have", "would have", "was"],
  "For a past condition that didn't happen, use \"If I had known... I would have.\""),
